==Venue==
[[Zayed Sports City Stadium]] in [[Abu Dhabi]] was announced as the venue for the final in May 2018, reprising its role as the final venue in [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup final|2009]], [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup final|2010]], and [[2017 FIFA Club World Cup final|2017]].<ref>{{cite news |last=McAuley |first=John |date=16 May 2018 |title=Al Ain ready to take on 'world class' teams after confirming place at Fifa Club World Cup |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/football/al-ain-ready-to-take-on-world-class-teams-after-confirming-place-at-fifa-club-world-cup-1.731083 |work=[[The National (Abu Dhabi)|The National]] |location=Abu Dhabi |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> It is the largest stadium in the United Arab Emirates and is primarily used by the [[United Arab Emirates national football team|Emirati national football team]].<ref>{{cite news |date=17 December 2018 |title=Ultimate guide to the eight stadiums used at the 2019 Asian Cup in the United Arab Emirates |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/asian-cup/ultimate-guide-to-the-eight-stadiums-used-at-the-2019-asian-cup-in-the-united-arab-emirates/news-story/d22f13ff7c8ad5d8e362d06ca74b3de6?nk=4571f879f99856fc09ea9029f90e8296-1545275496 |work=[[Fox Sports (Australia)|Fox Sports]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> Zayed Sports City Stadium hosted the [[1996 AFC Asian Cup|1996 Asian Cup final]] and is planned to host several matches in the [[2019 AFC Asian Cup]]. It is also featured on the 200 [[United Arab Emirates dirham|Dirham]] banknote.<ref>{{cite news |last=Parsar |first=Swapnaneel |date=24 November 2018 |title=Asian Cup 2019 stadiums: Your guide to the venues in the UAE |url=https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/asian-cup-2019-stadiums-venues-cities-uae/i9cohymbtcpc1k31gtusbqcr1 |work=[[Goal.com]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> The 43,000-seat stadium opened in 1980 and also hosted matches in the [[2003 FIFA World Youth Championship]] and the [[2013 FIFA U-17 World Cup]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Destinations: Abu Dhabi |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/cities/city/1051/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181220230855/https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/cities/city/1051/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 December 2018 |work=FIFA.com |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Reedie |first=Euan |date=27 May 2015 |title=Zayed Sports City: In a league of its own |url=https://gulfnews.com/sport/uae-sport/zayed-sports-city-in-a-league-of-its-own-1.1522806 |work=[[Gulf News]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref>

==Background==
Real Madrid qualified for the Club World Cup as winners of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]] by defeating [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the [[2018 UEFA Champions League Final|final]].<ref>{{cite news |last=McAuley |first=John |date=27 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid book return trip to UAE for Fifa Club World Cup after Champions League success |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/football/real-madrid-book-return-trip-to-uae-for-fifa-club-world-cup-after-champions-league-success-1.734276 |work=The National |location=Abu Dhabi |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> The club won three of the four previous editions of the Club World Cup: in [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup|2014]], [[2016 FIFA Club World Cup|2016]] and [[2017 FIFA Club World Cup|2017]].<ref>{{cite news |date=28 May 2018 |title=Real Madrid write new chapter in their rich European history |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/real-madrid-write-new-chapter-in-their-rich-european-history |work=FIFA.com |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> This was their fifth participation and third consecutive appearance in the tournament, both a record for European teams. The match was their fourth overall final (after [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2014]], [[2016 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2016]] and [[2017 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2017]]), tying the record with [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]]. The match also was their third consecutive final, extending their record set in the previous edition. The match was the 14th consecutive and overall final featuring a European team (with only the [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship Final|first final in 2000]] including no team from Europe), and the 8th overall and 5th consecutive final featuring a Spanish team, all extending the competition records. If Real Madrid were to win, they would become the outright record winners of the Club World Cup with four titles, breaking their tie with Barcelona for the record. A win would also extended the records for most consecutive titles for a team (3), most titles for a confederation (11 for UEFA), most consecutive titles for a confederation (6 for UEFA, breaking the tie set by European teams between [[2007 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2007]] and [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2011]]), most titles for a nation (7 for Spain), and most consecutive titles for a nation (5 for Spain).<ref name="RSSSF">{{cite web |last1=Leme de Arruda |first1=Marcelo |last2=Di Maggio |first2=Roberto |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fifawcc.html |title=FIFA Club World Cup |website=RSSSF.com |publisher=[[Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation]] |date=20 December 2018 |access-date=22 December 2018}}</ref>

Al Ain qualified for their first Club World Cup as winners of the [[2017–18 UAE Pro-League|2017–18 season]] of the [[UAE Pro-League]], the top-level league in the United Arab Emirates.<ref>{{cite news |date=16 May 2018 |title=Al Ain qualify for Fifa Club World Cup |url=https://gulfnews.com/sport/uae-sport/al-ain-qualify-for-fifa-club-world-cup-1.2222385 |work=[[Gulf News]] |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref> Al Ain was the first Emirati team to reach the Club World Cup final,<ref>{{cite news |last=McAuley |first=John |date=18 December 2018 |title=Fifa Club World Cup: Al Ain beat River Plate on penalties to reach historic final |url=https://www.thenational.ae/sport/2.2049/fifa-club-world-cup-al-ain-beat-river-plate-on-penalties-to-reach-historic-final-1.804186 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181219123344/https://www.thenational.ae/sport/2.2049/fifa-club-world-cup-al-ain-beat-river-plate-on-penalties-to-reach-historic-final-1.804186 |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 December 2018 |work=The National |location=Abu Dhabi |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref> as well as the second Asian team (after [[Kashima Antlers]] in 2016). The final was also the third final to feature the host representative (after [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] in 2000, [[Raja Casablanca]] in 2013 and Kashima Antlers in 2016). If Al Ain were to win, they would have become the first team outside of Europe and South America to win the Club World Cup, as well as the second host representative to win the tournament (after Corinthians in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|2000]]).<ref name="RSSSF"/>

The final was the second between an Asian and European team, after Real Madrid won against Kashima Antlers in the 2016 final. The final was also the third final between the host representative and a European team, both won by the European team, with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] winning against Raja Casablanca in [[2013 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2013]], in addition to the aforementioned 2016 final. The match was the fourth final not to feature a South American team after [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup Final|2010]], 2013 and 2016 (all of which the European teams won).<ref name="RSSSF"/>

