===Real Madrid===
As European champions, Real Madrid received a [[Bye (sports)|bye]] to the semi-final round, where they faced [[AFC Champions League|Asian]] champions [[Kashima Antlers]] of Japan. Kashima, who had defeated [[CONCACAF Champions League|CONCACAF]] champions [[C.D. Guadalajara|Guadalajara]], were defeated by Real Madrid in the 2016 final.<ref>{{cite news |date=16 December 2018 |title=Kashima wins to set up showdown against Real Madrid in Club World Cup semifinals |url=https://www.japantimes.co.jp/sports/2018/12/16/soccer/kashima-wins-set-showdown-real-madrid-club-world-cup-semifinals/ |work=[[Japan Times]] |agency=[[Reuters]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> Madrid defeated the Antlers 3–1 on a [[hat-trick (association football)|hat-trick]] scored by [[Gareth Bale]] over 11 minutes of play.<ref>{{cite news |date=19 December 2018 |title=Gareth Bale's 11-minute hat-trick fires Real Madrid into Club World Cup final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/dec/19/gareth-bale-hat-trick-real-madrid-kashima-antlers-club-world-cup |work=The Guardian |agency=[[Press Association]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> Bale scored in the 44th minute and added two goals in the 53rd and 55th minutes to open the second half; [[Shoma Doi]] scored a consolation goal for Kashima in the 78th minute after it was ruled onside by the [[video assistant referee]].<ref>{{cite news |date=19 December 2018 |title=Football: Hat-trick hero Bale fires Real Madrid into Club World Cup final |url=https://www.straitstimes.com/sport/football/football-hat-trick-hero-bale-fires-real-madrid-into-club-world-cup-final |work=[[Straits Times]] |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/brilliant-bale-bags-hat-trick-real-reach-final |title=Brilliant Bale bags hat-trick, Real reach final |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=19 December 2018 |access-date=22 December 2018}}</ref>

===Al Ain===
In the first round on 12 December, Al Ain defeated [[Team Wellington]] in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] following a 3–3 draw at their home stadium, [[Hazza bin Zayed Stadium]]. Wellington, a semi-professional club that qualified as the [[OFC Champions League]] champion, entered halftime with a 3–1 lead that was cut back by an equalising volley from [[Marcus Berg]].<ref>{{cite news |date=13 December 2018 |title=Football: Team Wellington handed shootout defeat at World Cup Club after leading 3–0 |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=12175963 |work=[[New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=18 December 2018 |archive-date=30 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930081131/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/sport/news/article.cfm?c_id=4&objectid=12175963 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The match remained scoreless after extra time and advanced to a penalty shoot-out, which Al Ain won 4–3 after five rounds after goalkeeper [[Khalid Eisa]] made two saves.<ref>{{cite news |date=12 December 2018 |title=Eisa: I never doubted our ability to qualify |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/eisa-i-never-doubted-our-ability-to-qualify |work=FIFA.com |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Cornwell |first=Alexander |date=12 December 2018 |title=Al Ain hit back from three behind, win on penalties |url=https://af.reuters.com/article/tunisiaNews/idAFL3N1YH4P2 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181219090854/https://af.reuters.com/article/tunisiaNews/idAFL3N1YH4P2 |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 December 2018 |publisher=[[Reuters]] |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref>

Al Ain advanced to face African champions [[Espérance Sportive de Tunis|Espérance de Tunis]] in the second round match, held three days later at Hazza bin Zayed Stadium. The team defeated Espérance 3–0 in an [[upset (sports)|upset]] that began with two goals scored in the opening 16 minutes.<ref>{{cite news |date=15 December 2018 |title=Fifa Club World Cup: African champions Esperance lose 3–0 to Al Ain |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/46581124 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref> Al Ain produced a larger upset in the semi-finals, defeating [[Copa Libertadores]] champions [[Club Atlético River Plate|River Plate]] in a penalty shoot-out to advance to the Club World Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |date=18 December 2018 |title=River Plate stunned by Al Ain in Club World Cup shootout |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/dec/18/river-plate-al-ain-club-world-cup-match-report |work=The Guardian |agency=Associated Press |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref> The match began with two early goals for River Plate scored by [[Rafael Santos Borré]] following an opening strike from Berg; after an equalising goal was disallowed by the [[video assistant referee]], [[Caio Lucas Fernandes]] scored for Al Ain in the 51st minute to draw the teams level at 2–2. After a scoreless extra time, aided by goalkeeper Essa's saves, Al Ain defeated River Plate 5–4 in a penalty shoot-out, its second of the competition,<ref name="Gulf-Finest">{{cite news |last=Hammond |first=Ashley |date=19 December 2018 |title=Mamic hails UAE football's finest hour as Al Ain stun River Plate |url=https://gulfnews.com/sport/football/mamic-hails-uae-footballs-finest-hour-as-al-ain-stun-river-plate-1.61021810 |work=Gulf News |access-date=19 December 2018}}</ref> with Essa making one save on River's [[Enzo Pérez]].<ref>{{cite news |date=18 December 2018 |title=River Plate fall to Al Ain on penalties in Club World Cup semifinal |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=529811 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |agency=Reuters |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=18 December 2018 |title=Club World Cup: River Plate 2–2 Al Ain (4–5 pens) |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/46610375 |publisher=BBC Sport |access-date=18 December 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/al-ain-advance-to-final-on-penalties |title=Al Ain outlast River to reach final with shoot-out win |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=18 December 2018 |access-date=22 December 2018}}</ref> The semi-final upset of River was called the "greatest achievement" in Emirati football history by Al Ain manager [[Zoran Mamić]].<ref name="Gulf-Finest"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
Real Madrid had 70&nbsp;percent of possession in the first half, taking advantage of Al Ain's defensive lapses to produce 11 shots.<ref name="Guardian">{{cite news |last=Unwin |first=Will |date=22 December 2018 |title=Real Madrid 4-1 Al Ain: Club World Cup final – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2018/dec/22/real-madrid-v-al-ain-club-world-cup-final-live |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=25 December 2018}}</ref> After a saved chance from Al Ain's [[Hussein El Shahat]], [[Luka Modrić]] opened scoring for Madrid in the 14th minute with a left-footed shot.<ref name="Marca">{{cite news |last=Bell |first=Arch |date=22 December 2018 |title=Real hit the jackpot in the final |url=https://www.marca.com/en/football/real-madrid/2018/12/22/5c1e83a4e5fdea4a6f8b4581.html |work=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]] |access-date=25 December 2018}}</ref> [[Caio Lucas Fernandes|Caio]] attempted to equalize a minute later, but was found to be [[Offside (association football)|offside]]. The half ended with a 1–0 lead for Madrid, with several later shots saved by Al Ain goalkeeper [[Khalid Eisa]].<ref name="NationalAE">{{cite news |last=Radley |first=Paul |date=22 December 2018 |title=Real Madrid claim record fourth Fifa Club World Cup with 4-1 victory over Al Ain |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/club-world-cup/real-madrid-claim-record-fourth-fifa-club-world-cup-with-4-1-victory-over-al-ain-1.805453 |work=The National |location=Abu Dhabi |access-date=25 December 2018}}</ref>

Madrid opened the second half with another series of attacks while retaining possession of the ball, finding its second goal in the 60th minute on a long-distance strike by [[Marcos Llorente]].<ref name="Marca"/> The pace of the match slowed as Al Ain looked to find a consolation goal, with Caio taking advantage of a defensive mistake by [[Sergio Ramos]] but unable to score against goalkeeper [[Thibaut Courtois]].<ref name="Guardian"/> Ramos responded by scoring in the 79th minute, a header on a [[corner kick]] taken by Modrić after a counter-attack, to give Real Madrid a three-goal lead. Six minutes later, a free kick taken by Caio found left-back [[Tsukasa Shiotani]], who scored Al Ain's only goal of the match.<ref name="NationalAE"/> In [[stoppage time]], an [[own goal]] was scored by Al Ain's [[Yahia Nader]] on a cross by Madrid substitute [[Vinícius Júnior]]; it was Madrid's final goal to extend their lead to 4–1.<ref name="Marca"/>

